# lisp-f: f folds Clojure, Common Lisp, Racket and Scheme (#1352). Inside a top-level form f folds
# the whole form, from its first line to its closing paren, and lands on that first line; f there
# unfolds it. The file is written here, not in the project, which every other scenario counts the
# files of.
merl
wait Open a file (fuzzy)
run mkdir -p lisp && printf '(ns demo.core)\n\n(defn greet [name]\n  (let [s (str "hi " name)]\n    (println s)\n    s))\n\n(def answer 42)\n' > lisp/core.clj
key q
wait merl exited: 0
merl lisp/core.clj
wait A core.clj
key :
type 5
key Enter f
wait lisp/core.clj  3:1
key f
wait (println s)
key q
wait merl exited: 0
